    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "\"transitional neighborhood\" and \"up-and-coming\" are definitely the industry standard, i get that. \n\nbut let's go a level deeper. i've had clients ask me point-blank about the 'longevity' of the luxury in these areas. for Oak Ridge, they assume the luxury is protected by the community itself—it's just a given that the neighborhood will stay the same. \n\nbut with Riverside, they worry that the \"vibrant\" energy they're buying into might actually dilute the actual value or the \"class\" of the area over time as it continues to transition. it's like they're buying into a moment in time rather than a permanent status. \n\nis there a way to use language that suggests the \"culture\" of Riverside is something that *enhances* the premium nature of the property, rather than something that might eventually overwhelm it? like, how do we frame that \"eclectic mix\" so it feels like a curated, high-end lifestyle choice for the buyer, rather than just a sign of a neighborhood in flux?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
